<%= render layout: "guides/frame", locals: { guide: @guide } do %>
  <p>
    Compounding pharmacies often assume DSCSA is about someone else&rsquo;s supply chain. Part of that is right,
    and part of it isn&rsquo;t. Here&rsquo;s where the line falls for a 503A pharmacy.
  </p>

  <section>
    <h2>Compounded preparations aren&rsquo;t DSCSA &ldquo;product&rdquo;</h2>
    <p>
      DSCSA&rsquo;s tracing rules apply to &ldquo;product&rdquo;: a prescription drug in a finished dosage form.
      The statute&rsquo;s definition expressly excludes a drug compounded in compliance with section 503A or
      503B. So the preparations you compound and dispense don&rsquo;t carry DSCSA transaction information,
      and you don&rsquo;t trace them under DSCSA.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>The manufactured drugs you buy still are</h2>
    <p>
      Most compounding pharmacies also buy and dispense commercially manufactured prescription drugs, and some
      use them as ingredients. When you take ownership of those, you&rsquo;re a dispenser receiving product, and
      the usual dispenser obligations apply, exemption or not:
    </p>
    <%= render "guides/applies_today" %>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Are you a small dispenser?</h2>
    <%= render "guides/qualify" %>
    <p>
      If you qualify, FDA has deferred the enhanced, electronic, package-level requirements until
      <%= DscsaDeadline.label %>. If you don&rsquo;t, they already apply to the manufactured products you handle.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Practical steps</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Separate the two flows on paper: what you compound, and what you buy finished. Only the second needs
        DSCSA transaction records.</li>
      <li>Check that every supplier of finished product is an authorized trading partner, including the one you
        found during a shortage.</li>
      <li>Write a one-page suspect-product procedure; it applies to finished product you receive.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      This guide is about 503A pharmacies. 503B outsourcing facilities have their own federal obligations;
      check FDA&rsquo;s outsourcing-facility guidance.
    </p>
  </section>
<% end %>
